                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi ℝ
                            %mo +
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^+
                    as a terminal coalgebra
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#smooth_real_numbers"} Smooth real numbers
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#other_definitions"} Other definitions
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Topologies"} Topologies
              %li
                %a{:href => "#generalisations"} Generalisations
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em real number
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/number"} number
            that may be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+completion"} approximated
            by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/addition"} addition
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplication"} multiplication
            induced from the rational numbers, real numbers form a
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+completion"} completion
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordered+field"} ordered field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℚ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            of rational numbers: the result of adjoining to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℚ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suprema"} suprema
            for every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} inhabited
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+set"} bounded subset
            with respect to the natural
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order"} ordering
            of rational numbers.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            of real numbers also carries naturally the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+structure"} structure
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            and as such
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            is called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            also known as
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum"} the continuum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+field"} topological field
            and as such is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+space"} uniform completion
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℚ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            equipped with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+value"} absolute value
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric
          %p
            Together with its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+products"} cartesian products
            – the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+spaces"} Cartesian spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
            – the real line
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            is a standard formalization of the idea of
            = succeed "." do
              %em continuous space
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            is modeled on these Cartesian spaces. These, in turn are standard models for the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            in particular in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            (see
            = succeed ")," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+physics"} classical physics
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics"} geometry of physics
            for more on this.
          %h2#history History
          %p
            The original idea of a real number came from
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            = succeed "," do
              %em point on a line
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line"} line
            understood as the abstract idea of the object that a pencil and a ruler draw on a piece of paper. (More precisely, given two distinct points on the line, called
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
            between the points and the real numbers.)
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclid"} Euclid
            (citing
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eudoxus"} Eudoxus
            = succeed "s;" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/number"} number
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eudoxus+real+number"} Eudoxus real number
          %p
            A big project of the 19th century (at least in hindsight) was the ‘arithmetisation of analysis’: showing how real numbers could be defined completely in terms of rational numbers (and the desired classes of functions on them could be defined in terms of the general point-set notion of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            = succeed "'s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Dedekind"} Richard Dedekind
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+cuts"} Dedekind cuts
            = succeed "'s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Cantor"} Georg Cantor
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+sequences"} Cauchy sequences
          %p
            A more modern approach is instead to characterise the properties that the set of real numbers must have and to prove that this is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+the"} categorical
            (unique up to a unique bijection preserving those properties). Then the important result of the 19th-century programme is simply that this is consistent (that there exists at least one such set). One can even use
            = succeed "'s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Hilbert"} Hilbert
            = succeed "'s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alfred+Tarski"} Tarski
          %p
            Exactly how to define or characterise real numbers is still important in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            with its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers+object"} real numbers object
            and the examples below.
          %h2#definitions_and_characterizations Definitions and characterizations
          %p
            There are two basic approaches possible: to define what a
            %strong real number
            is as a mathematical object, or to define the
            %strong real line
            as a specific object in some previously known
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
          %h3#the_terminal_archimedean_ordered_field The terminal Archimedean ordered field
          %p
            There is a well-known algebraic (more or less) characterisation of the real line as the ‘terminal Archimedean ordered field’ (cf.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Richman08"} Richman08
          %ul
            %li
              A
              %strong field
              is well known in algebra; if it matters, we mean a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+field"} Heyting field
            %li
              An
              %strong ordered field
              means a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strictly+totally+ordered"} strictly totally ordered
              field.
            %li
              An
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Archimedean+ordered+field"} Archimedean ordered field
              is an ordered field satisfying the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Archimedean+property"} Archimedean property
            %li
              An Archimedean ordered field is
              %strong terminal
              if it is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
              in the category of Archimedean ordered fields.
          %p
            We speak of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/the"} the
            such field because it is unique up to unique
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            by definition of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicative mathematics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{R}
            by fiat via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
            of the real numbers as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Archimedean+ordered+field"} Archimedean ordered field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+coalgebra"} terminal coalgebra
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+functor"} identity endofunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \mapsto X
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Archimedean+ordered+fields"} Archimedean ordered fields
          %p
            If
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+sets"} quotient sets
            exist and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequence+algebras"} sequence algebras
            of Archimedean ordered fields exist, then it is provable that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{R}
            is Cauchy complete. From the definition of terminal object,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{R}
            is an algebra of the endofunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \mapsto \mathcal{C}(X)
            in the category of Archimedean ordered fields which takes Archimedean ordered fields
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to the Archimedean ordered field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}(X)
            of equivalence classes of Cauchy sequences in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \mapsto \mathcal{C}(X)
            in the category of Archimedean ordered fields is a Cauchy complete Archimedean ordered field.
          %p
            Similarly, if
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+sets"} power sets
            of Archimedean ordered fields exist, then it is provable that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{R}
            is Dedekind complete. From the definition of terminal object,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{R}
            is an algebra of the endofunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \mapsto \mathcal{D}(X)
            in the category of Archimedean ordered fields which takes Archimedean ordered fields
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to the Archimedean ordered field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}(X)
            of two-sided Dedekind cuts in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \mapsto \mathcal{D}(X)
            in the category of Archimedean ordered fields is a Dedekind complete Archimedean ordered field.
          %h3#Dedekind Dedekind cuts
          %p
            Consider two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} inhabited
            subsets,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+set"} countable
            unbounded
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+linear+order"} dense linear order
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℚ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            (the set of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mn 10
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}[1/10]
            (the set of
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decimal+fractions"} decimal fractions
          %ul
            %li
              If
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in L
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in L
              for some
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \gt a
            %li
              If
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in U
              for some
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \lt b
            %li
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \lt b
              are rational numbers, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in L
              or
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in U
            %li
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in L
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in U
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \lt b
          %p
            We may define a
            %strong Dedekind real number
            to be such a pair, which is also called a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+cut"} Dedekind cut
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \coloneqq (L,U)
            is a Dedekind cut, then we write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \lt x
            to mean that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in L
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \lt b
            to mean that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in U
          %p
            We may approximate a Dedekind cut
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            as closely as we like by applying (*) as often as necessary. This will be only finitely often, for any fixed positive level of approximation, given initial upper and lower bounds (which exist since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            are inhabited).
          %p
            See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+completion"} Dedekind completion
            for more.
          %h3#cauchy_sequences Cauchy sequences
          %p
            Classically, a real number can be given by an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+sequence"} infinite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+sequence"} Cauchy sequence
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decimal+fractions"} decimal fractions
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 10
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}[1/10]
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mn 10
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0.099999
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0.10000
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1/10 = 0.099999... = 0.10000...
          %p
            so we need to specify an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
            on the Cauchy sequences. Thus,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            is constructed as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subquotient"} subquotient
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+set"} function set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 10
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}[1/10]^{\mathbb{N}}
          %p
            We can generalise this to any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+sequence"} Cauchy sequence
            of rational numbers, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            is constructed as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subquotient"} subquotient
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+set"} function set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℚ
                      %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}^{\mathbb{N}}
          %p
            This construction is equivalent to the construction by Dedekind cuts, at least assuming the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+countable+choice"} weak countable choice
            principle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} AC
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{AC}_{\mathbb{N}2}
            (which also follows from
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
            and traditional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            (which accepts
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+choice"} countable choice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+languages"} internal languages
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nets"} nets
          %p
            See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+real+number"} Cauchy real number
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+Cauchy+real+number"} generalized Cauchy real number
            for more.
          %h3#the_initial_sequentially_modulated_cauchy_complete_archimedean_field The initial sequentially modulated Cauchy complete archimedean field
          %p There is an algebraic (more or less) characterisation of the real line as the ‘initial sequentially modulated Cauchy complete archimedean field’. This can be interpreted as follows:
          %ul
            %li
              A
              %strong field
              is well known in algebra; if it matters, we mean a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+field"} Heyting field
            %li
              An
              %strong ordered field
              means a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+total+order"} strictly totally
              ordered field.
            %li
              An
              %strong archimedean field
              is an ordered field satisfying the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/archimedean+property"} archimedean property
            %li
              An archimedean field is
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequentially+modulated+Cauchy+complete"} sequentially modulated Cauchy complete
              if all
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+sequences"} Cauchy sequences
              in the field with a
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modulus+of+convergence"} modulus of convergence
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/converge"} converge
            %li
              A sequentially modulated Cauchy complete archimedean field is
              %strong initial
              if it is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial object
              in the category of sequentially modulated Cauchy complete archimedean fields.
          %p
            The initial sequentially modulated Cauchy complete ordered field results in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HoTT+book+real+numbers"} HoTT book real numbers
          %h3#the_dedekind_complete_ordered_field The Dedekind complete ordered field
          %p There is a well-known algebraic (more or less) characterisation of the real line as the ‘Dedekind complete ordered field’, or sometimes the ‘Dedekind complete archimedean field’. This can be interpreted as follows:
          %ul
