            However, we note that the proof is valid in weak
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            with a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers+object"} real numbers object
          %p
            Even weaker foundations are possible if one allows the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying+set"} underlying set
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            to be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+class"} large
          %p
            However, if we are working in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicative mathematics
            with multiple
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universes"} universes
            in the foundation, the Cauchy real numbers would no longer be unique up to unique isomorphism, but rather there would be a set of Cauchy real numbers for each universe.
          %h3#as_an_archimedean_tarski_group As an archimedean Tarski group
          %p There is a characterisation of the real line as the ‘complete archimedean Tarski group’ due to Alfred Tarski. This can be interpreted as follows:
          %ul
            %li
              An
              %strong abelian group
              is well known in algebra.
            %li
              An
              %strong linearly ordered abelian group
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
              with a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+order"} linear order
            %li
              A
              %strong densely linearly ordered abelian group
              is an linearly ordered abelian group that is also a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+linear+order"} dense linear order
            %li
              A
              %strong Tarski group
              is a densely linearly ordered abelian group with a point
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \lt 1 + 1
            %li
              An
              %strong archimedean Tarski group
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tarski+group"} Tarski group
              satisfying the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/archimedean+property"} archimedean property
            %li
              An archimedean Tarski group is
              %strong complete
              if it is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+completion"} Dedekind-complete
          %p
            See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tarski%27s+axiomatization+of+the+real+numbers"} Tarski's axiomatization of the real numbers
            for more information.
          %h3#the_locale_of_real_numbers The locale of real numbers
          %p
            Consider
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+relations"} binary relations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable"} countable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited"} inhabited
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+linear+order"} dense linear order
            without endpoints, such as the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
          %ul
            %li
              If
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ≥
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \geq b
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∼
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \sim b
            %li
              If
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ≥
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∼
                      %mi c
                      %mo ≥
                      %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \geq b \sim c \geq d
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∼
                      %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \sim d
            %li
              If
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∼
                      %mi b
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∼
                      %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \sim b \gt c \sim d
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∼
                      %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \sim d
            %li
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \sim c
              whenever
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \lt b
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \lt d
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∼
                      %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \sim d
          %p
            The collection of all such relations form a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame"} frame
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale+of+real+numbers"} locale of real numbers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localic+completion"} localic completion
            of the rational numbers.
          %p
            We may then define a
            %strong localic real number
            to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point+of+a+locale"} point
            of this locale.
          %p
            This agrees with the notion of Dedekind real number, even in very weak (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicative
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
          %p
            See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale+of+real+numbers"} locale of real numbers
            for more.
          %h3#unit_intervals Unit intervals
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coalgebra+of+the+real+interval"} unit interval
            of the real numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]
            could be constructed as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+coalgebra+of+an+endofunctor"} terminal coalgebra of an endofunctor
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intervals"} intervals
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo ,
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{R},0,+,-,1,\cdot,\lt)
            be an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordered+field"} ordered field
            where
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \lt 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monotone"} monotone
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:[0,1]\to \mathbb{R}
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(0) = 0
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(1) = 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            of real numbers is the initial such ordered field.
          %p
            See also:
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dyadic+interval+coalgebra"} dyadic interval coalgebra
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decimal+interval+coalgebra"} decimal interval coalgebra
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+interval+coalgebra"} rational interval coalgebra
          %h3#_as_a_terminal_coalgebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^+
            as a terminal coalgebra
          %p
            The positive real line
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^+
            may be characterized as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coalgebra+for+an+endofunctor"} coalgebra for an endofunctor
          %p
            Let
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pos"} Pos
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Pos
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  U\colon Pos \to Set
          %p Consider the endofunctor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Pos
                  %mo →
                  %mi Pos
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F_1\colon Pos \to Pos
          %p
            defined as the
            %span.newWikiWord
              ordinal product
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/ordinal+product"} ?
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi ω
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F_1\colon X \mapsto \omega \cdot X,
          %p
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Pos
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega \in Pos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega \cdot X
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(\omega) \times U(X)
            with the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lexicographic+order"} lexicographic order
          .un_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              The terminal coalgebra of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_1
              is order isomorphic to the non-negative real line
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mo +
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^+
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              This is theorem 5.1 in (
              %a{:href => "#PavlovicPratt"}> Pavlovic–Pratt 1999
              ).
            %p
              There are many ways of setting up this description of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mo +
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^+
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo +
                    %mo →
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ⋅
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^+ \to \omega \cdot \mathbb{R}^+
              chosen. Here is one: there are evident poset isomorphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo +
                    %mo ≅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^+ \cong [1, \infty)
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                      %mo ≅
                      %msub
                        %mi ℕ
                        %mrow
                          %mo ≥
                          %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo :
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega \cong \mathbb{N}_{\geq 2} = \{n \in \mathbb{N}: n \geq 2\}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi α
                    %mo ,
                    %mi β
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 2
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\alpha, \beta): [1, \infty) \to \mathbb{N}_{\geq 2} \cdot [1, \infty)
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha(x)
              is the smallest integer strictly greater than
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi β
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo −
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta(x) = 1/(\alpha(x) - x)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi β
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_n = \alpha(\beta^n(x))
              gives a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continued+fraction"} continued fraction
              representation of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              in the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 0
                    %mo −
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mn 1
                        %mo −
                        %mfrac
                          %mn 1
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi a
                              %mn 2
                            %mo −
                            %mi …
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = a_0 - \frac1{a_1 - \frac1{a_2 - \ldots}},
            %p
              and the resulting bijection
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi ℕ
                        %mrow
                          %mo ≥
                          %mn 2
                      %mo ×
                      %msub
                        %mi ℕ
                        %mrow
                          %mo ≥
                          %mn 2
                      %mo ×
                      %mi …
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [1, \infty) \to \mathbb{N}_{\geq 2} \times \mathbb{N}_{\geq 2} \times \ldots
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a_0, a_1, \ldots)
          %p
            Another way, which circumvents the use of isomorphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo +
                  %mo ≅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^+ \cong [1, \infty)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ≅
                    %msub
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega \cong \mathbb{N}_{\geq 2}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi α
                  %mo ,
                  %mi β
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo +
                  %mo →
                  %mi ω
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\alpha, \beta): \mathbb{R}^+ \to \omega \cdot \mathbb{R}^+
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha(x)
            is the floor of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi β
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta(x) = 1/(1 - x + \alpha(x)) - 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi β
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_n = \alpha(\beta^n(x))
            gives a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continued+fraction"} continued fraction
            representation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            in the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 0
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi a
                            %mn 1
                          %mo +
                          %mfrac
                            %mn 1
                            %mrow
                              %mn 1
                              %mo +
                              %mfrac
                                %mn 1
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi a
                                    %mn 2
                                  %mo +
                                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = a_0 + \frac1{1 + \frac1{a_1 + \frac1{1 + \frac1{a_2 + \ldots}}}},
          %p
            and the resulting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo +
                    %mo →
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ×
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ×
                    %mi …
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}_+ \to \omega \times \omega \times \ldots
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a_0, a_1, \ldots)
          %p
            There are more and similar characterizations along these lines. An interesting application of the coalgebraic views is that they allow defining morphisms between the reals. A coalgebraic version Dedekind cuts or Conway numbers makes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            into a compact category where the addition arises as the biproduct. The real vector spaces are the
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Pavlovic"} Pavlovic 2020
          %h3#smooth_real_numbers Smooth real numbers
          %p
            The
            %strong smooth real numbers
            (say in a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+topos"} smooth topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordered+local+ring"} ordered local ring
            rather than an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordered+field"} ordered field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nilpotent"} nilpotent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimals"} infinitesimals
            in the smooth real numbers. Nevertheless, the quotient of the smooth real numbers by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ideal"} ideal
            of non-invertible elements is one of the ordered field of real numbers as defined above.
          %h3#other_definitions Other definitions
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eudoxus+real+number"} Eudoxus real number
            %li
              real numbers defined in terms of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radix+notation"} radix notation
          %h2#Topologies Topologies
          %p
            There are alternative topologies on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            sometimes considered:
          %ul
            %li
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+topology"} discrete topology
              (not very interesting),
            %li
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order+topology"} order topology
              or
              %span.newWikiWord
                absolute-value topology
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/absolute-value+topology"} ?
              (the usual topology of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
            %li
              the
              %span.newWikiWord
                upper-interval topology
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/upper-interval+topology"} ?
              or the
              = succeed "," do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  lower-interval topology
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/lower-interval+topology"} ?
            %li
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lower+semicontinuous+topology"} lower semicontinuous topology
              or the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/upper+semicontinuous+topology"} upper semicontinuous topology
            %li
              the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-topology"} K-topology
          %p
            Another variant of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            as a topological space is the
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/long+line"}> long line
              \.
          %h2#generalisations Generalisations
          %p
            The term ‘real number’ was originally introduced to indicate that one is
            %em not
            considering the generalistion to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            or other kinds of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypercomplex+numbers"} hypercomplex numbers
          %p
            The
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+real+number"} extended real number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ±
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pm\infty
            as well as the real numbers; one may speak of
            %em finite numbers
            or
            %em bounded numbers
            to indicate that one is not considering this extension.
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lower+real"} Lower reals
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/upper+reals"} upper reals
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MacNeille+real+number"} MacNeille reals
            are related generalisations studied in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            they are (at least if bounded) the same as ordinary real numbers; one may speak of
            %em located numbers
            to indicate that one is not considering such extensions.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surreal+number"} Surreal numbers
            and the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperreal+number"} hyperreal number
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonstandard+analysis"} nonstandard analysis
            are two ways to include
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+number"} infinite
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+number"} infinitesimal
            versions of real numbers (besides the trivial case of
            = succeed ");" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ±
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pm\infty
            %em standard numbers
            to indicate that one is not considering such extensions (although the precise meaning of ‘standard’ depends on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe"} universe
            that one is working in).
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descriptive+set+theory"} descriptive set theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Baire+space+of+irrational+numbers"} Baire space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^{\mathbb{N}}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schroeder-Bernstein+theorem"} Schroeder-Bernstein theorem
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^{\mathbb{N}}
            are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} isomorphic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^{\mathbb{N}}
            can still be thought of as the set of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irrational+numbers"} irrational numbers
            = succeed "." do
              %em restriction
          %p
            %span.newWikiWord
              Floating-point numbers
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/floating-point+number"} ?
            are often used in computer programming to represent real numbers, but they do not behave very well; one may speak of
            %em infinite-precision numbers
            to indicate that one's programming environment models ‘
            %a{:href => "http://math.fau.edu/richman/html/mm2.htm"}>
              %em real
              real numbers
            ’.
          %p
            As mentioned above, the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adic+number"} adic numbers
            for various
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+numbers"} prime numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            are variations on the theme of real numbers; real numbers may be thought of as
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
                \-adic numbers
            %em
              characteristic-
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              numbers
            since they are based on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+field"} prime field
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic"} characteristic
            (although it makes more sense to get analogues of complex numbers than of real numbers).
          %p
            Finally, one can consider points on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative
            line instead of the usual
            = succeed "." do
              %em commutative numbers
          %p
            So in summary, this page is about the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                real, finite, located, standard, analytic, infinite-precision,
                = succeed "-adic," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
